# Instructional Artifact Acceptance Gate

This rule applies whenever Cursor creates, edits, reviews, validates, exports, or reports on an instructional artifact.

Canonical standard:

`docs/qa/universal-instructional-artifact-qa-standard.md`

## Non-negotiable rules

- File generation is not completion.
- Do not call an artifact finished, complete, ready, classroom-ready, fully validated, print-ready, approved, or final unless the full acceptance standard supports that claim.
- Use the eight-stage validation sequence:
  1. source validation
  2. content-priority validation
  3. cross-artifact alignment
  4. educational suitability
  5. visual and layout QA
  6. technical QA
  7. classroom simulation
  8. final acceptance report
- Render and inspect every page and slide.
- Multi-artifact packages require a cross-artifact alignment table.
- Use Teacher AI Workstation validators when available.
- Missing or unavailable validator coverage produces WARN for tooling confidence.
- Never claim a check passed when it was not run.
- FAIL blocks delivery.
- WARN must be explained and requires teacher review.
- Numeric quality scores never override FAIL.
- Preserve PASS/WARN/FAIL semantics.
- No automated score replaces instructional judgment.
- No source content may be invented or presented as verified without support.
- No answer key may be accepted without comparison to the student artifact.
- No printable DOCX, PPTX, or HTML artifact may be considered pagination-validated without authoritative rendered or PDF inspection.

## Required final report

Every instructional-artifact completion report must include:

| Category | Required status |
| --- | --- |
| Source validation | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Content-priority validation | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Cross-artifact alignment | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Educational suitability | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Visual and layout QA | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Technical QA | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Classroom simulation | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Tooling confidence | PASS/WARN/FAIL |
| Overall status | PASS/WARN/FAIL |

List all warnings, blocking issues, files inspected, validators run, and validation gaps.

## Completion language

Use only the approved language in the canonical standard.

Overall PASS is the only state that permits an unqualified claim that a package is ready for classroom use.
